<!-- SHOW, DON'T TELL -->
<section class="wrap chapter" id="show" aria-labelledby="h-show">
  <div class="lens-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Show, don't tell</p>
    <h2 id="h-show">The sign-up form was the first lesson</h2>
    <p>Brindle Lake's first citizen developers use an automation before anyone explains what one is.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scrolly">
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step" data-step="0" id="s-show-0"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Registering</p>
        <h3>An ordinary sign-up</h3>
        <p>Staff fill in a sign-up form to join a first session. Each person gets a personalized confirmation.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="1" id="s-show-1"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Behind the form</p>
        <h3>An automation does the work</h3>
        <p>The flow<span class="gloss" data-aud="gen"> (a set of automated steps that runs when something happens)</span> takes each response, checks the calendar, routes the person to a session and sends the confirmation.</p>
        <p data-aud="pro">In automation terms: a form-response trigger, a calendar lookup, a condition that picks the session, and an email action for the confirmation.</p>
        <p class="if-reg">The panel traces your own registration. Tap any part of your confirmation to see which step wrote it.</p>
        <p class="if-unreg">The panel shows an example. <button type="button" class="linkbtn" data-goto-try>Register at the top</button> and it traces your own sign-up instead.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="2" id="s-show-2"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Keeping it accurate</p>
        <h3>When the schedule changes, so does the flow</h3>
        <p>Whenever a session moves, the flow gets updated too, so each person still lands in the right one.</p>
        <p class="if-reg">In the panel, your session moves, in a fictional schedule change, and the flow re-routes you.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="3" id="s-show-3"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The reveal</p>
        <h3>The mechanism, shown in the session</h3>
        <p>The session opens by taking the sign-up apart and showing how it worked.</p>
        <p>The reveal comes first, before any slides, because everyone in the room has already used it.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="4" id="s-show-4"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The invitation</p>
        <h3>Find the same pattern in your own job</h3>
        <p>People see the pattern working, then see how it works. The last step is to look for the same opportunity in their own work.</p>
        <p data-aud="gen">This is the moment someone goes from using a tool to building with it.</p>
        <p data-aud="pro">In citizen development terms, this is where a user becomes a maker: someone who builds for their own team.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
