<!-- THE FRONT DOOR -->
<section class="wrap chapter" id="front-door" aria-labelledby="h-fd">
  <div class="lens-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">The front door</p>
    <h2 id="h-fd">Support organized around the next step</h2>
    <p>In my experience, citizen developers get further when support answers what they are trying to do next.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="scrolly">
    <div class="steps">
      <div class="step" data-step="0" id="s-fd-0"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Starting point</p>
        <h3>Tools without users</h3>
        <p>Everyone on Brindle Lake's staff has a no-code automation tool in their office software. Almost nobody opens it, though branch staff have plenty of repetitive work. That gap is one place citizen development can start.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="1" id="s-fd-1"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The question</p>
        <h3>From “where is the document?” to “what do I do next?”</h3>
        <p>The staff intranet is organized like a filing cabinet. It helps people find files. It doesn't help them take the next step.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="2" id="s-fd-2"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">The rebuild</p>
        <h3>Four pages, each one a goal</h3>
        <p>The rebuild swaps categories for pages named after goals: try your first flow, find a pattern, ask for help, and share what you built.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="3" id="s-fd-3"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">Repository to task</p>
        <h3>Built around the decisions people make</h3>
        <p>The structure follows the decisions people are making. The owners' categories no longer decide where things live.</p>
        <p data-aud="pro">In information architecture terms: task-based rather than topic-based navigation, with each page labelled by the goal a person arrives with.</p>
      </div></div>
      <div class="step" data-step="4" id="s-fd-4"><div class="step-in">
        <p class="kicker">One step further</p>
        <h3>Connect the build journey to its checks</h3>
        <p>Each stage, from idea to launch, can point to the check it needs. A builder's one-page brief then travels with the build, so nobody writes the same story twice for each review.</p>
        <p data-aud="gen">Citizen development needs checks too. This is one way to put them where the builder already is.</p>
        <p data-aud="pro">This is one way to handle the governance side of citizen development: guardrails that travel with the maker, instead of gates each builder has to find on their own.</p>
      </div></div>
    </div>
    <div class="graphic">
      <div class="panel">
        <div class="sec">
          <div class="ia">
